WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.160
Back in June at AI DevCon in London, we had several talks

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:06.200
about the part that tends to get left out of the demo.

3
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:07.480
The bill.

4
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:09.000
Let's first hear from Amit

5
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.400
Kushwaha, a principal solutions architect at Nvidia, on the two things

6
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.760
that decide how much of an agent's work you actually pay for,

7
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.920
whether your context is cached, and whether the request

8
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.920
comes back to the machine holding that cash.

9
00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.080
My name is Amit.

10
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.080
I'm a solutions architect at Nvidia,

11
00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:30.080
and we live in the

12
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:31.640
Defense Department.

13
00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:35.000
So looking at that workload, there are few optimizations

14
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.560
that are already kind of present in the production systems.

15
00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:42.600
And these are very critical to make that workload work.

16
00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.440
Again, going back to kind of the current

17
00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:48.080
setup that

18
00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:52.040
I shared in the previous slide, in the first turn,

19
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.320
this is your user input and you get an output in the second term.

20
00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.600
This whole thing could be cached.

21
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.040
And this is the optimization

22
00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.920
that I'm talking about for most of the agent workloads.

23
00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:09.360
Caching becomes very, very important because what you are doing is pilot.

24
00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.360
By the time you are at the 40th turn,

25
00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:17.280
a lot of context has been created, but you don't need to do all the work.

26
00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.080
If you are caching your output inputs properly, then you only need to do

27
00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:26.680
work on the new tokens that are being generated in this whole workflow.

28
00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:31.360
So at the end of kind of this, this fourth term, for example,

29
00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.800
most of this is cached and you don't have to do any work on it.

30
00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.160
The new part is only where you run your GPUs and actually to make the work,

31
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.920
if the caching was not enabled, you can imagine

32
00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.640
a lot more work needs to be done, and there's consequences

33
00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:49.240
on one performance on time to first token and different kinds of metrics.

34
00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:54.280
So caching becomes kind of a must have in most of these agentic developments.

35
00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.200
And as you see, the

36
00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.480
vast majority of output input tokens by the end could be cached

37
00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.520
because it's just building up on the previous display.

38
00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:09.640
The caching itself is not the full story,

39
00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.960
especially when you are serving these models on multiple replicas.

40
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:18.960
Your terms have to kind of get on the right replica.

41
00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:23.560
For example, if you turn one in, the previous slide landed on replica one,

42
00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:27.560
and this is just an example of you could have a model serving multiple times.

43
00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:29.200
So these are three replicas

44
00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:32.200
of the same model that you are using neural network things.

45
00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:35.520
So turn one ended up on replica K.

46
00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.480
If you do a round robin kind of a setup

47
00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.480
the turn you might end up on some other replica.

48
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:45.080
But this replica does not have the cash that we talked about.

49
00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:48.320
So the other thing that becomes very important in a

50
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.360
kind of setup is to make sure that your turns

51
00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.120
land on the same replica where your cash is stored.

52
00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:59.920
So this is what is kind of shown here in the cave where routing,

53
00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:03.920
where turn one lands on replica one, we make sure that the turn

54
00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:07.680
two also lands on replica one, so that we can leverage the cache

55
00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.000
that is stored already on that side.

56
