00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:44.120
to reach for a frontier model when you genuinely need it.

102
00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:48.080
most of my day now when I'm doing technical work

103
00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:52.720
is that I'm reviewing these specs, and I find the two lines in the spec

104
00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:56.760
where the agent got it wrong, or is making a poor design decision, or

105
00:05:56.920 --> 00:06:01.960
is unaware of some other area of the code, and you just comment on those two lines,

106
00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:05.240
and then it then becomes fairly mechanical

107
00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:09.240
for the agent to get the code change all the way through to

108
00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.920
to being close to being ready to merge.

109
00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:19.000
So and then the pull requests that come out of it are quite nice,

110
00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:22.120
because we put quite a lot of effort into telling the agent

111
00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:25.720
to take screenshots of changes that it's made.

112
00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:28.400
So I quite often review pull requests

113
00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:31.040
now by just looking at whether I like the change.

114
00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:33.320
And that's quite powerful.

115
00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:36.960
And you can also link, we also link automatically to the spec

116
00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:41.040
that was used to to construct it.

117
00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:46.800
Now I want to talk briefly about token costs, privacy and Donald Trump.

