00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:45.040
Opus 4.8 if you've got something really hard.

134
00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:48.760
But for all of the tedious stuff, you should be using local models

135
00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:52.040
where you just have to pay for the electricity, in my opinion.

136
00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:55.160
to close, John Groetzinger at Cisco, on

137
00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:58.360
spending your humans as carefully as you spend your tokens.

138
00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:00.440
When a knowledge base article changes,

139
00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:04.240
a model, rewrites the matching skill and runs the evaluations.

140
00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:07.360
Small changes that pass release themselves.

141
00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:10.440
A person only gets pulled in for the big ones.

142
00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:14.160
So a lot of people will say wikis over docs go to die.

143
00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:16.600
And of course, largely I do agree with that

144
00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:18.480
because people just don't really maintain docs.

145
00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:22.360
But when you have a strong culture like we do, our docs are not really dying

146
00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:23.840
or actually seeing the opposite.

147
00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:26.280
Our docs are becoming more important to ground the agents,

148
00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:30.200
but what we're seeing is that a lot of people are building agentic systems

149
00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:34.280
that blindly try to use any content, or they'll have agents that build

150
00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:37.520
their own memories on supporting stuff, using that, and then the memories drift

151
00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:41.040
from the content, and then you end up having some answer come out to a customer.

152
00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:44.720
And that's just completely unaligned with the article that it says

153
00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:47.920
it uses a source, and then everyone just loses faith in the entire system.

154
00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:48.160
Right?

155
00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:49.720
And that's the problem we've been struggling with

156
00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:50.920
for the last couple of years.

157
00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:51.720
So we're trying to figure out

158
00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:54.880
how do we keep these more in sync in a way that we can actually trust it.

159
00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:56.280
And that's what the system

160
00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:59.280
that these engineers built is that when the article changes,

161
00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:00.520
we kind of pipeline that, right.

162
00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:04.160
Think of like a GitHub action triggering when we update a knowledge base article

163
00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:08.480
that goes into a pipeline with an LLM that reviews the changes, determines

164
00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:12.240
if it's a minor change, moderate major, and then makes the change for itself

165
00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:15.120
to update its skill. And then it runs the evals.

166
00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:17.280
If the evals pass, great, everything's good.

167
00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:19.800
If it's a minor change, just changing a typo or wording

168
00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:22.880
and the evils pass, I don't really need to have my human gate.

169
00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:25.680
That new version, just let it automatically release.

170
00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:27.200
But if you're doing a more complex

171
00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:30.200
change, a major change, maybe you're introducing a new topic.

172
00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:32.640
Maybe that's something that you need some more quality control.

173
00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:34.480
And that's where you insert your human right.

174
00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:38.000
Your humans either go far on the left or far on the right, but in between

175
00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:41.800
you should not be wasting your time, should not be caring about a text diff.

176
00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:43.200
If your engineers are reviewing

177
00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:45.800
text diffs on a skill, you are wasting a lot of time.

178
00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:48.800
That is just unnecessary, at least in my experience.

179
00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:51.840
So new topic means new eval, right?

180
00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:53.440
You need to build a new evaluation.

181
00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:56.480
Of course you can let the LM do that, but you need to really tell it what success

182
00:09:56.480 --> 00:09:59.520
looks like in order for it to build a good evaluation.

